    {"id": "F1", "category": "coverage", "severity": "medium", "decl": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 19, "paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6", "explanation": "Only the quantitative bound is formalized. Existence, uniqueness, the Fourier-series formula, and zero-mean solution are not part of this statement. Whether `inverse d f` is the paper's N⁻¹ is not visible.", "polarity": "lean weaker", "probe_idea": "State a lemma that `slice (inverse d f) p` has Fourier coefficients F̂(k)/(2πi vt·k) and solves N φ = slice f p."},
    {"id": "F2", "category": "definition-mismatch", "severity": "medium", "decl": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 21, "paper_ref": "(8.19)", "explanation": "The paper bounds by ‖F‖_{C^{m+4}}, all derivatives up to order m+4. Lean hypotheses bound only f and the pure x-jet of order |w|+5 (plus swapped), i.e. a different regularity count (+5 vs +4) and fewer derivatives. The hypotheses are only on [0,1]², so periodicity must carry them. If these are insufficient the theorem would be false, but kernel-checked so presumably fine; it is a different statement from the paper, and plausibly stronger in hypotheses economy but not comparable.", "polarity": "unclear", "probe_idea": "Compare via a corollary: C^{m+5} norm bound of F implies hypotheses hold with C = ‖F‖, and the conclusion yields (8.19)-type estimate."},
    {"id": "F3", "category": "junk-value", "severity": "medium", "decl": "mixedLossConstant", "line": 14, "paper_ref": null, "explanation": "The constant uses a `tsum` of (weight k^4)⁻¹. If the family were not summable, or weight vanished, the constant would be 0 or junk and the statement would be false or meaningless. Summability is not shown in the excerpt.", "polarity": "unclear", "probe_idea": "Prove `Summable (fun k : Frequency => (weight k ^ 4)⁻¹)` and `0 < mixedLossConstant r`."},
    {"id": "F4", "category": "assumption", "severity": "low", "decl": "norm_derivativeWord_inverse_le", "line": 20, "paper_ref": "Lemma 8.6", "explanation": "The hypotheses `ContDiff ℝ ∞ f` and `Periodic f` are additional (periodicity is implicit in T²). The zero-mean condition on F is not an explicit hypothesis, so it is presumably built into `inverse` (which must subtract or ignore the zero mode); this cannot be checked from the excerpt. `Direction d` is a parameter absent from the paper's lemma.", "polarity": "unclear", "probe_idea": "Inspect `inverse`, `Source`, `Periodic`, `Direction` definitions."},
    {"id": "F5", "category": "other", "severity": "low", "decl": "derivativeWord", "line": 9, "paper_ref": "(8.19)", "explanation": "The paper's C^m_y norm refers to y-derivatives only, while Lean bounds arbitrary words of coordinate partials (both x and y per Bool), and the conclusion involves the full plane. So Lean concerns different derivative sets and a pointwise bound at arbitrary Y; the role of x vs y on T² (vt·∂y operator) is unclear.", "polarity": "unclear", "probe_idea": "Unfold `coordinatePartial` and check which coordinates are differentiated; test w = [true,false]."}
